.TH CHTTPSERVER 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
chttpserver \- overview of the chttpsvr HTTP/1.1 server
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A chttpsvr serves HTTP/1.1 over plain TCP, over TLS or on a Unix
domain socket. It routes each request by method and path pattern, with
named path parameters, middleware chains and sub-routers that own a
path prefix. A route either gets the whole body in memory
(chttpsvr_register_handler(3)) or reads the body as it arrives
(chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler(3)).

You usually create the server (chttpsvr_create(3) or
chttpsvr_construct(3)), register routes and middleware, start it with
a chttpsvr_config_t (chttpsvr_start(3)), wait with
chttpsvr_engine_wait(3) and then destroy it (chttpsvr_destroy(3)).
.fi
.SS "Handles"
.nf
chttpsvr is an opaque value handle and not a pointer, and
CHTTPSVR_NULL (0) means no server. The library checks every handle
before it touches the server, so a call through an invalid or
destroyed handle fails cleanly. A destroy of a stale handle, a second
destroy that races the first, and a destroy from a handler or a
middleware of the same server stop the process.

A sub-router (chttpsvr_subrouter(3)) belongs to its server. A
chttpsvr_req and a chttpsvr_resp belong to one request, and they and
every pointer that a chttpsvr_req_* function gives you are valid only
while the handler or middleware call runs.
.fi
.SS "The engine"
.nf
All the servers of a process share one reactor, the engine. The first
chttpsvr_start(3) starts it, and the destroy of the last running
server starts its teardown, which finishes on a background thread
after the destroy returns. chttpsvr_engine_wait(3) blocks until the
engine has stopped completely. Call it before main() returns, before
dlclose(3) of the library, and before you release an allocator that
chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(3) installed.
chttpsvr_engine_stop(3) asks the running engine to drain and stop. It
is the only async-signal-safe function of this module, and it is the
safe way for a handler to shut its own server down.
.fi
.SS "Threads"
.nf
The reactor only accepts connections and parses request heads, and it
never runs application code. A buffered route runs on the worker pool
of its server and a streaming route on a separate streaming pool, so
a slow streaming client can take up at most the streaming pool. Both
kinds of handler, and every middleware, may block.

No thread waits for a slow client of a buffered route. A body that
stops arriving, or a response that meets a full socket, parks its
connection without a thread, and any free worker continues it later.
A request that finds the worker queue full gets 503 with Retry-After.
.fi
.SS "Limits and rejections"
.nf
A malformed or unsupported request is refused before any middleware
or handler sees it, for example with 505 for an HTTP major version
other than 1, 400 for an invalid Host and 501 for a transfer coding
other than chunked. The limits of chttpsvr_config_t bound the header
size, the body size, the memory that bodies hold, the time and the
rate of each transfer, and the number of connections, and every
duration field is a uint64_t count of microseconds. After a rejection
the server lingers for at most 2 seconds before it closes the
connection, so that a client which is still sending its body reads
the response instead of a reset.
.fi
.SS "Routing"
.nf
The most specific mounted prefix that owns a path handles the request
on its own. Its middleware runs for every path under it, and a path
that none of its routes matches gets its 404 or 405. Inside one
router the routes are tried in the order you registered them. A
CHTTP_GET route also serves HEAD. Each router caps its middleware
chain at 32 entries. Paths and parameter values are percent-decoded
and their dot segments are kept, so never use one as a file name
without checking it.
.fi
.SS "Ownership and memory"
.nf
A server, its routes and its pools come from the allocator of
chttpsvr_create_mp(3), and the engine uses the allocator of
chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs(3). The server derives its own
logger from the clog handle that the create receives, and that handle
stays the caller's. chttpsvr_destroy(3) frees everything the server
owns except about 100 bytes for each sub-router, which stay until the
process exits so that a sub-router pointer is always safe to pass.
The library never frees a value that chttpsvr_req_set_context(3)
attaches to a request.
.fi
.SS "Signals"
.nf
The library installs no signal handler and never changes the
disposition of SIGPIPE. Every write to a connection uses MSG_NOSIGNAL,
so a client that goes away ends only its own connection.
.fi
.SS "fork(2)"
.nf
Fork before you create and start any server, so that each process
builds its own, or call exec() right after fork(). A child that keeps
running without exec() while a server from before the fork is live is
not supported. See ccollections(7).
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Every function that takes a server handle may be called from any
thread, and routes and middleware may be registered before or after
the server starts. A handler must not destroy, stop or restart its
own server, and it must not call chttpsvr_engine_wait(3); it calls
chttpsvr_engine_stop(3) and returns instead. chttpsvr_stop(3) is not
async-signal-safe. A chttpsvr_req and a chttpsvr_resp are used only
on the thread that runs their handler.
.fi
